Open a phone's new-tab links in the tab the user is already on.

Tabs live behind a grid on a phone, so a link with target=_blank opens one the
user never sees, and back does not return to where they came from because the
new tab has no history. Navigating in place keeps both.

Only link navigations are affected, and deliberately so. A target=_blank link
comes through OpenURLFromTab, and since Chrome 88 such a link implies noopener
-- nothing on the page holds a handle to the window that would have appeared,
so nothing breaks by not creating it. window.open() takes another path
entirely: it needs a browsing context and arrives through AddNewContents, so
an OAuth or payment popup that reads the window it opened is untouched. Where
it cannot be known, the new tab stays, which is what was asked for.

Renderer-initiated only, so the shell's "open in new tab", the newTab command
and restoring a closed tab all still open one.

Phones only, read from ui_family at the moment of the click, so unfolding a
foldable changes the behaviour with it and no preference is involved.

On device the tab still opened and nothing was logged, so the paragraph above
is in doubt: the click may be reaching AddNewContents instead, which would
mean the renderer creates the window itself rather than asking. Both doors now
log the disposition and whether the renderer started it. Those two LOG lines
are temporary and come out once the answer is in.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

